description.narrative:
1. Practice - building CSO's and government actors’ capacities, strengthening networks, alliances and long-term partnerships with other organizations or entities that are working towards similar goals, influencing public policies and/or legislation and collaborating with existing infrastructure; 
2. Socially - developing leadership paths for children and young people (especially for girls). The impact of the project is felt by and lasts among the participants and end beneficiaries if they are involved in making decisions about the project. The steering committee at the heart of this element. The project will support the strengthening of skills that participants can use or benefit from throughout their lives, such as youth leadership, focusing on the structural causes of social and gender inequalities;
3. Economically: throughout the implementation Plan International and potentially partners will analyze and put into practice methods to generate new funds to maintain some or all of the project activities. For this, Plan International Brazil has a team available to identify opportunities and generate fundraising strategies for the sustainability of the project. Alternative sources of funding other than grants are also possible in some possibilities: for example, fundraising events, individual donors, sales of products or services, etc.
